UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 2, Lesson 1: Dimensions of health', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Health has interconnected dimensions (commonly physical, mental/emotional, social, and sometimes spiritual/cultural). A change in one dimension can affect others; strategies should notice the links.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nGiven a short scenario, label two dimensions affected and propose one strategy that helps both.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: dimensions named accurately; strategy realistic and linked to both.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTreating health as only sport fitness, or proposing unsafe “fixes.”","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently analyse two scenarios for dimensional links and write a balanced wellbeing recommendation for each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Health has interconnected dimensions (commonly physical, mental/emotional, social, and sometimes spiritual/cultural). A change in one dimension can affect others; strategies should notice the links.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PD4-1"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently analyse two scenarios for dimensional links and write a balanced wellbeing recommendation for each. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using health dimensions on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Treating health as only sport fitness, or proposing unsafe “fixes.”. Correction: Strong answer: dimensions named accurately;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating health as only sport fitness, or proposing unsafe “fixes.” Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating health dimensions on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with health dimensions, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about health dimensions with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for health dimensions. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for health dimensions. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","dimensions named accurately;","Treating health as only sport fitness, or proposing unsafe “fixes.”","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Given a short scenario, label two dimensions affected and pro